{"caseId":"OD2-HO-OBVWRONG-1","answers":[{"itemId":"Q1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":88,"evidenceIds":["X10","E1","E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"Q2","answer":"supported","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["X10","E3","E5"]},{"itemId":"Q3","answer":"contradicted","confidence":82,"evidenceIds":["X10"]},{"itemId":"Q4","answer":"contradicted","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E3","X10"]},{"itemId":"Q5","answer":"contradicted","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["X10","E6"]},{"itemId":"Q6","answer":"causal","confidence":78,"evidenceIds":["X10","E3","E5"]},{"itemId":"Q7","answer":"association-only","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E4","E1"]},{"itemId":"Q8","answer":"contradicted","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["X10","E3"]},{"itemId":"Q9","answer":"harmful","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["X10","E2"]},{"itemId":"Q10","answer":"preferred","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["X10","E5"]},{"itemId":"Q11","answer":"premature","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E4","E1"]},{"itemId":"Q12","answer":"acceptable","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["X10"]},{"itemId":"Q13","answer":"harmful","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E4","E3"]},{"itemId":"Q14","answer":"sufficient","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["X10","E3"]}],"brief":"What is going on: the Kestria buffering is almost entirely the new smart-TV app version 5.2, which went live on 9 October, the same day the CDN cutover finished. Playback on the same Tessaline CDN is normal for every other client: the 5.1 TVs with auto-update off, the web player, and the iOS/Android apps all sit at about 0.6–0.7% rebuffering. Smart-TV 5.2 sits at about 5.9% across all three ISPs, and it starts video about 1.5 s faster, which points to a changed adaptive-streaming setting. The CDN is therefore not the main cause, and the handball launch does not explain it because the rise began before it.

How sure: fairly confident (roughly 80%) on the app cause. This comes from one ad hoc warehouse query, not a controlled test. Cancellations are not yet shown to be caused by buffering, since the 1 October price rise is a rival explanation.

Next: do not send the SLA notice, withhold fees, or move traffic back to Corvane, since that would punish the vendor for a fault it did not cause. Do not mute the alert. Ask engineering to revert 5.2's adaptive-streaming settings to 5.1 on Kestria TVs and pause the wider 5.2 rollout. Confirm the fix with the 5.2 rebuffering rate. Add app version and device breakdowns to the dashboard, accepting the roughly six-week rebuild."}
